 of Shimazu Yoshihisa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-nariakira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimazu-nariakira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimazu Nariakira</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimazu Nariakira was daimyo of the Satsuma Domain from 1851 to 1858 and one of the most forward-looking lords of late Tokugawa Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-takahisa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimazu-takahisa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimazu Takahisa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimazu Takahisa was a Sengoku-period lord who reunited the rival branches of the Shimazu clan and the province of Satsuma by about 1550, ruling from Kagoshima.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-toshihisa"><span class="enc-name">Shimazu Toshihisa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimazu Toshihisa was a samurai commander of the Shimazu clan of Satsuma, the third of the four brothers who led the clan's conquest of most of Kyushu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihiro"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihiro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimazu Yoshihiro</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimazu Yoshihiro was a samurai general of the Shimazu clan of Satsuma, brother of Shimazu Yoshihisa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihisa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimazu-yoshihisa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimazu Yoshihisa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimazu Yoshihisa was head of the Shimazu clan of Satsuma from 1566.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimla</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimla, formerly Simla, is a city in the lower Himalaya and the capital of Himachal Pradesh, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimoda"><span class="enc-name">Shimoda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimoda is a port town at the tip of the Izu Peninsula in Shizuoka Prefecture, Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimonoseki.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimonoseki</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimonoseki is a city at the south-western tip of Honshu, Japan, on the Kanmon Strait facing Kyushu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shimosa-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shimosa-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shimōsa Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shimōsa was an old province of Japan, covering the north of present-day Chiba Prefecture and part of Ibaraki Prefecture, east of Edo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shin-arahan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shin-arahan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shin Arahan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shin Arahan was a Theravada Buddhist monk who, according to Burmese chronicles, converted King Anawrahta of Pagan in the 11th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sh